criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
Release PR behaviour, proven by `check-release-preview`: release-plz 0.3.172 and cargo-semver-checks 0.51 run `release-plz update` in a scratch copy of the working tree (system temp directory, local tags only, push URL disabled) with `v1.0.0` tagged on the base commit:

- a `docs:` commit alone changes nothing (version stays `1.0.0`, clean tree);
- `fix(dtcg_runtime_derive): …`: workspace version and both `Cargo.lock` entries become `1.0.1`; the root `CHANGELOG.md` keeps exactly one `# Changelog` header and gets a new first section `## [1.0.1] - YYYY-MM-DD` listing the derive commit; no `crates/*/CHANGELOG.md` appears; the derive crate's dev-dependency on `dtcg_runtime` stays path-only; `cargo metadata` resolves;
- `feat(dtcg_runtime): …` whose body states a break and ends with a `Migration: RayX: …` footer (no `!`, no `BREAKING CHANGE:`): both crates `1.1.0` (minor), the section shows the line `Migration: RayX: …`, `cargo metadata` resolves.

`docs/releasing.md` (maintainer page; every code block language-tagged) covers:

- what a release is: tag `vX.Y.Z` on `main` plus a GitHub Release whose notes are that version's `CHANGELOG.md` section; both crates share one version, one tag and one changelog, `dtcg_runtime_derive` never gets its own tag or release; nothing goes to crates.io although the manifests stay packageable;
- how it happens: release-plz keeps a release pull request open on every push to `main`; review it (version, changelog); merge it; the `release` job tags and creates the Release. The first release `v1.0.0` was tagged without a pull request (git-only initial release);
- the commit-type table (`fix`/`perf` → patch, `feat` → minor; `docs`, `test`, `ci`, `build`, `chore`, `refactor` alone → no release, listed in the next one) and the **1.x policy**: the owner is the only consumer, so no commit is marked breaking (no `!`, no `BREAKING CHANGE:`); a breaking change is a `feat` (or `fix`) whose body states the break and whose `Migration:` footer names each consumer's migration (RayX, RayX.TokenEditor, files), which the changelog prints; `semver_check = false` because release-plz cannot cap a major bump; the policy ends when a second consumer appears (then `!` and major bumps return);
- before merging a release PR: the proposed version is still `1.x` (a `2.0.0` proposal means a stray `!`/`BREAKING CHANGE:`; reword that commit, force push allowed, instead of merging) and the PR changes only `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock` and `CHANGELOG.md`;
- `CHANGELOG.md` is written by release-plz: no hand edits in feature commits; a line that reads badly is fixed on the release PR right before merging (release-plz replaces a release PR with human commits when `main` moves on);
- what CI a release PR runs (`ci.yml`, CI-1);
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN`: the shared fine-grained PAT (resource owner `rayx-ui`, the four library repositories, Contents and Pull requests read/write), repository or organization secret, why the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` is not enough, its silent-expiry failure mode (red Release-plz runs, no release PR, no tag) and rotation;
- the `v*` tag ruleset: tags cannot be moved or deleted, a bad release is fixed forward with a new version;
- the by-hand fallback: version in `[workspace.package]` and both `[workspace.dependencies]` requirements, `cargo update --workspace`, a `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` section, commit and push, `git tag vX.Y.Z`, `git push origin vX.Y.Z`, `gh release create`; release-plz continues from that tag;
- consumers pin `dtcg_runtime = { git = "https://github.com/rayx-ui/RayX.Dtcg", tag = "vX.Y.Z" }` and bump by hand;
- the push policy (`git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main`, `git push --force-with-lease origin main`, no feature branches or pull requests except release PRs).

Verified by `check-release-docs`.
